/* Bhasaly - Religious studies content, JAVANESE (jv).
   Translated from faith/ne.js. The English [2] and the scripture reference [3]
   of every paragraph are byte-identical to the Nepali source; only the native
   [0] and the romanization [1] are Javanese.

   Vocabulary follows the Javanese Bible (Kitab Sutji), verified against its
   Yokanan 3 text: Gusti Allah God, Gusti Yesus Jesus, Panjenengane the
   honorific third person used of God and Jesus, Putrane Gusti Allah Son of
   God, dosa sin, kaslametan salvation, Juru Slamet Rescuer, salib cross,
   Roh Suci Holy Spirit, prajanjian covenant, nabi prophet, murid disciple,
   kurban sacrifice, Padaleman Suci temple, Cempe lamb, pracaya to believe,
   Kratoning Allah the kingdom of God, urip langgeng eternal life, Kabar
   Becik good news.

   REGISTER - the same call the Sinhala pack made, for the same reason. The
   Kitab Sutji is formal and krama-inflected, but lang/jv.js teaches NGOKO
   because that is what is actually spoken. Putting liturgical Javanese in
   front of an un-churched reader would work against the whole point, so this
   pack uses accessible Javanese with plain grammar, and carries respect the
   way Javanese Christians do in ordinary speech: through the honorific NOUNS
   and verbs for God and Jesus (Panjenengane, rawuh, ngandika, seda, wungu,
   miyos, paring) rather than through krama throughout. The Lord's Prayer in
   jprayer is the one deliberate exception - it is quoted in the krama form
   Javanese Christians actually pray.

   ROMANIZATION follows lang/jv.js: a PRONUNCIATION RESPELLING, where /a/ in
   an open final syllable becomes o and the change spreads leftward through
   open /a/ syllables (basa -> boso, pracaya -> procoyo, aksara -> aksoro).
   Every rom word was checked against a lexicon built from lang/jv.js itself
   (scratchpad/faith/jv_romcheck.py). Integrated Bible names take the rule
   (Maria -> Mario, Musa -> Muso), modern geographic loans do not (Asia,
   Afrika). Paraphrase only - never copied translation text.
   The two Nepal references in the source are localised to Java. */

